+# Final review: cluster 6 (ipc-rs), epic hg-k4l
+
+## Scope and inputs
+
+- Cluster: ipc-rs; file src/ipc.rs; slice slices/000041.diff (+25 lines, no deletions).
+- Captured source: base 0dbe4772f4cd541e6b5ecaa1dd55b9a72f62c784, head efc21b65eb225578d3a623b29f23b3688849ea8b; both verified with `git rev-parse --verify <sha>^{commit}`. Caller checkout observed at HEAD efc21b65 with a clean status; no changes made to it.
+- Spec: docs/superpowers/runs/2026-11-05-seat-failure-manager-seats/2026-11-05-seat-failure-manager-seats-design.md (via `bd show hg-k4l`), sections 4.1, 4.2, 4.4, the Key abstractions entry for CommandError / IpcErrorCode, and the Requirement baseline.
+
+## What the cluster changes
+
+- Adds `IpcErrorCode::Integrity` (serde snake_case -> wire string `"integrity"`) with a doc comment stating spec 4.1 semantics and the no-bump compatibility consequence.
+- Adds unit test `integrity_code_serde`: round-trips an `IpcResult::Error { code: Integrity, .. }`, asserts `status = "error"` and `code = "integrity"` on the wire, and asserts an unknown code is a decode error (closed enum, no `#[serde(other)]` fallback).
+- Adds unit test `ipc_version_is_unchanged`: pins `IPC_VERSION == 1`.
+
+## Spec conformance
+
+- Spec 4.1 asks for `IpcErrorCode::Integrity` without bumping `IPC_VERSION`. Delivered: the variant exists, `IPC_VERSION` stays 1, and a test pins it (Global Constraint "No IPC_VERSION bump" is met).
+- Spec 4.1 compatibility rule (b), new daemon + old CLI: the old CLI's `IpcErrorCode` has no catch-all variant, so `serde_json` rejects `"integrity"` inside `IpcResponse`; `read_frame` surfaces that as `FrameError::Json`. That matches the accepted degradation, and the doc comment says the same. The closed-enum assertion in the new test proves this property on the current type, so a later `#[serde(other)]` would trip the test instead of silently changing the skew contract.
+- Spec 4.1 compatibility rule (a), new CLI + old daemon: an old daemon never emits `integrity`; adding a variant does not change how existing codes decode. Nothing in this file needs to change for that.
+- Placement: the Key abstractions section assigns "which IpcErrorCode maps to which exit code" to cli::daemon_error. ipc.rs only adds the wire code and holds no classification logic, which keeps it inside its stated role ("this file owns only the envelope and framing").
+
+## Baseline trace (B4)
+
+- B4 (named non-transient integrity error, distinct from the transient case) is the only baseline row this cluster touches. The wire half lives here, and it is delivered: `Integrity` is its own code, separate from `Unavailable`, and the doc comment calls it not transient.
+- Production path at head, checked only to confirm that the variant has real producers and consumers: `CommandError::integrity` / `CommandError::from_store` (src/daemon/registry.rs:60-71) -> server reply mapping `Err(CommandError { code, message }) => IpcResult::Error { code, message }` (src/daemon/server.rs:272) -> client decode to `ClientError::Remote { code: Integrity, .. }` (covered by src/daemon/client.rs test `integrity_reply_is_remote_integrity`) -> `IpcErrorCode::Integrity` arm in src/cli/daemon_error.rs:95 (exit 6). The variant is wired end to end. Whether each B4 reference kind actually raises it (seat_reply, instructions, the three silent skips) and how it reaches exit 6 is outside this cluster: that belongs to the bootstrap, daemon and cli cluster reviewers and to the seam reviewer.
+- No other baseline row (B1-B3, B5-B14, S1) has code in src/ipc.rs.
+
+## Code quality
+
+- The change is minimal and consistent with the surrounding variants (same derive set, same rename rule, doc comment style matching `StillRunning`).
+- Matches over `IpcErrorCode` elsewhere are exhaustive (src/cli/daemon_error.rs lists every variant with no wildcard), so the compiler forces any future code to be classified. The new variant is handled there.
+- The tests are cheap and assert the properties the spec relies on (wire spelling, closed enum, version pin). I did not run them here: building needs the ht-build toolbox and would write into the caller's target/, which this review treats as read-only. By reading, the assertions match the type definitions at head.
